# MIPS bitfield-extract decode: a shift count computed from two instruction fields can go negative

VERDICT line first, then the liveness token `CODEX_MIPSEXT2_20260910`.

## READ ONLY THESE TWO PATHS. DO NOT LIST OR SEARCH ANY DIRECTORY.

```
C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\GXEMUL-SEC\src\cpus\cpu_mips_instr.c
C:\DocumentNoSnc\CC\GXEMUL\_scratchpad\mips64_volII.txt
```

*(The second is a `pdftotext -layout` extraction of the MIPS64 Volume II instruction-set
reference. It sits at the PROJECT root, which is one level ABOVE the repository root — a previous
run of this brief gave the path relative to the repo, could not find it, and spent its whole
budget walking a large artifacts directory instead of answering. Everything else you need is
inlined below; there is nothing to look for.)*

This is ordinary arithmetic-correctness review of an instruction decoder in an open-source CPU
emulator written in C99, of the same kind as a compiler front-end range check. Three unrelated
2021-vintage copies of `src/` exist elsewhere on this machine and would give line numbers off by
hundreds — the path above is the one under review.

**Please check the premises rather than confirming them.** A recent review here had seven of nine
readers confirm a premise that was stated wrongly, and on this particular item an earlier review
already corrected my title: I had blamed plain `DEXT`, and **plain `DEXT` turns out to be fine**.
So the claims below have been wrong once already.

## THE TWO EXECUTE HANDLERS, VERBATIM AND COMPLETE

```c
/*  ext:  Extract bitfield.   arg[2] = (msbd << 5) + lsb  */
X(ext)
{
	int msbd = ic->arg[2] >> 5, lsb = ic->arg[2] & 0x1f;
	int size = msbd + 1;
	uint32_t rs = reg(ic->arg[1]);
	uint32_t x = (rs << (32-lsb-size)) >> (32-lsb-size);
	reg(ic->arg[0]) = (int32_t) (x >> lsb);
}

/*  dext:  Extract bitfield (64-bit).   arg[2] = (msbd << 6) + lsb  */
X(dext)
{
	int msbd = ic->arg[2] >> 6, lsb = ic->arg[2] & 0x3f;
	int size = msbd + 1;
	uint64_t rs = reg(ic->arg[1]);
	uint64_t x = (rs << (uint64_t)(64-lsb-size)) >> (uint64_t)(64-lsb-size);
	reg(ic->arg[0]) = x >> lsb;
}
```

## THE DECODER, VERBATIM — and note the THREE opcodes share ONE block

```c
		case SPECIAL3_EXT:
			{
				int msbd = rd, lsb = (iword >> 6) & 0x1f;
				ic->arg[0] = (size_t)&cpu->cd.mips.gpr[rt];
				ic->arg[1] = (size_t)&cpu->cd.mips.gpr[rs];
				ic->arg[2] = (msbd << 5) + lsb;
				ic->f = instr(ext);
				if (rt == MIPS_GPR_ZERO)
					ic->f = instr(nop);
			}
			break;

		case SPECIAL3_DEXT:
		case SPECIAL3_DEXTM:
		case SPECIAL3_DEXTU:
			{
				int msbd = rd, lsb = (iword >> 6) & 0x1f;
				if (s6 == SPECIAL3_DEXTM)
					msbd += 32;
				if (s6 == SPECIAL3_DEXTU)
					lsb += 32;
				ic->arg[0] = (size_t)&cpu->cd.mips.gpr[rt];
				ic->arg[1] = (size_t)&cpu->cd.mips.gpr[rs];
				ic->arg[2] = (msbd << 6) + lsb;
				ic->f = instr(dext);
				if (rt == MIPS_GPR_ZERO)
					ic->f = instr(nop);
			}
			break;
```

An `ic->f = instr(reserved);` arm exists a few lines above this switch and is this file's own way
of declining an encoding it will not decode.

## MY ARITHMETIC, WHICH I WANT CHECKED RATHER THAN CONFIRMED

`rd` and the `lsb` field come from an instruction word, so `32-lsb-size` and `64-lsb-size` are
`int` expressions whose value depends on program input. Where that value is **negative** the shift
is undefined in C99, and in practice the count is reduced modulo the width, so the extraction
quietly returns the wrong bits rather than reporting anything.

I count **496 of 1024 field pairs per opcode**, three times over:

| opcode | pos | size | architectural constraint | pairs outside it |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `EXT`   | `lsb` 0..31     | `msbd+1` 1..32   | `0 < pos+size <= 32`  | 496 |
| `DEXT`  | `lsb` 0..31     | `msbd+1` 1..32   | `0 < pos+size <= 32`  | **0 — fine** |
| `DEXTM` | `lsb` 0..31     | `msbd+33` 33..64 | `32 < pos+size <= 64` | 496 |
| `DEXTU` | `lsb+32` 32..63 | `msbd+1` 1..32   | `32 < pos+size <= 64` | 496 |

**Questions:**

1. **Are those four rows what MIPS64 actually specifies?** In particular: is `DEXTM`'s size
   `msbd + 33` (which is what `msbd += 32` followed by `size = msbd + 1` computes), and is
   `DEXTU`'s pos `lsb + 32`? If the field adjustments are correct, say so — I need to know whether
   only the RANGE is unchecked, or whether the adjustments are wrong as well.
2. **Is plain `DEXT` genuinely fine?** My argument is that `lsb <= 31` and `size <= 32` give
   `64-lsb-size >= 1` always. Please check it; this row's title was already wrong once in the
   other direction.
3. Reproduce or refute the **496** figure for each of the three. Show the counting.
4. What does the specification say results for the out-of-range encodings are — UNPREDICTABLE,
   reserved, or something defined? Quote the sentence with its line number from the extracted
   text above. Cite only what you have actually read there.

## WHAT I WOULD LIKE DESIGNED

5. **A range check: where does it belong and what should it say?** My reading is that it belongs
   in the DECODER rather than the handler, and that **placement within the block matters**: the
   `DEXT`/`DEXTM`/`DEXTU` check has to sit AFTER the two `+= 32` adjustments, where the local
   variables have become the real `pos` and `size`, and one check there then covers all three
   opcodes with `DEXT` never able to trip it. **Is that right?** Please write the exact text for
   both blocks.
6. **What should the decoder DO with an encoding outside the range?** This project keeps an
   explicit menu for such a decision — leave as is; decode to a no-op silently; decode to a no-op
   with a single bounded diagnostic; accept it anyway; raise the architectural Reserved
   Instruction exception; invent storage and accept; or implement the semantics properly. Please
   look at what THIS FILE already does with an encoding it will not decode, and choose the member
   the file's own precedent supports rather than the one that argues best. Is `instr(reserved)`
   the right answer, and what does it do to the running program?
7. **Does the handler need a check too?** A decoder check covers the ordinary path. Is there any
   other route to `X(dext)` or `X(ext)`?
8. **What is the smallest edit to your check that still compiles and lets an out-of-range pair
   through?** Name the encoding and the wrong value it yields, so I can add that case to the
   regression suite. And specifically: **could a check placed BEFORE the `+= 32` adjustments look
   right and be wrong?** If so I want that case in the suite too.
9. **One change or three?** House rule: one site per change; a second joins only if it is in the
   same file, the same class so that **one design covers both**, and is covered by the **same test
   with no new oracle**. `EXT` is 32-bit with its own handler; `DEXTM`/`DEXTU` share a block and a
   handler. Please rule, with the reason.
10. **Is there a runnable reproduction?** This project will not change what it cannot reproduce.
    Can an ordinary program reach this on an unmodified in-tree MIPS machine — is `EXT` in the
    base instruction set of a machine this emulator boots, or MIPS32r2-and-later only? Name the
    machine and the `-E` selector if one exists, and say plainly if none does.

